    "available_at": "2026-11-18T18:37:13.854341+00:00",
    "body": "DHL Global Forwarding has leased a Boeing 747 freighter aircraft and crew to provide its first-ever dedicated service for the India market, creating regular capacity connecting China, India and Europe.\nThe freight management arm of DHL Group ( XETRA: DHL ) announced Monday that it has launched its own-controlled freighter service between Hong Kong, Navi Mumbai airport in India and Liège airport in Belgium to support growing demand between the regions. Flights will operate twice weekly, giving shippers in China direct capacity into the Mumbai region, while opening additional capacity from India to Europe. DHL Forwarding will use its ground network to move shipments from Liège airport to destinations across the continent.\nDHL Global Forwarding secured the capacity through DHL Aviation, the company’s in-house cargo airline, but the flights are being operated by an undisclosed partner carrier. DHL Aviation operates and manages a fleet of nearly 300 aircraft, including Boeing 777 and 767 freighters, but relies on former joint venture partner Polar Air Cargo and other carriers for Boeing 747 airlift.\nAdding a scheduled, dedicated service supports DHL Forwarding’s broader expansion in the Indian market.\nIndia is one of the fastest-growing air freight markets in Asia, supported by strong growth in manufacturing and exports. International air cargo volumes are growing strongly because of accelerating e-commerce demand, supply chain diversification away from China and the growth of advanced manufacturing capacity, with high-value and time-sensitive products leading the way. East Asia is a major source of components for products made in India. On the export side, India is shipping large amounts of pharmaceuticals to Europe and North America, including higher-value complex generics, biosimilars and next-generation therapies. India has also emerged as an alternative source of active pharmaceutical ingredients, according to Boeing’s latest air cargo forecast.\nThe DHL service is expected to support customers in industries such as life sciences and healthcare, technology and electronics, engineering and advanced manufacturing.\nThe Boeing 747 freighter offers about 100 metric tons of capacity per flight. DHL Global Forwarding is initially targeting around 25 to 30 metric tons per flight for the India sectors, or about 55 tons per week, with potential to grow as demand develops.\n“India is a strategic market for DHL Global Forwarding and an increasingly important part of our global air freight network,” said Henk Venna, global head of air freight, in a news release. “With this new dedicated service, we are strengthening connectivity between China, India and Europe and providing our customers with reliable capacity and greater access to key growth markets. This expansion reflects our long-term commitment to India and to supporting our customers as their trade flows grow.”\nDHL Global Forwarding said it plans to further expand its dedicated connectivity between India and Europe with a new Bengaluru-Europe service in March.\n“This is not a one-off addition. It is part of our broader ambition to build a stronger air freight bridge between India and the rest of the world. The new Mumbai service, followed by Bengaluru-Europe, demonstrates our confidence in the Indian market and our ambition to grow alongside our customers,” said Venema.\nDHL is not alone among integrated logistics companies building up capabilities in India. FedEx in August announced plans to invest $150 million to outfit a proposed air hub in Delhi, India. It previously said it would invest $250 million to equip a new air terminal at Navi Mumbai International Airport .\nFedEx invests $250M for dedicated parcel hub at new Mumbai airport",
